---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, href } from '../data/site';
import { decomposition, measurements, threads, run, pct, share } from '../data/measurements';
import CostBars from '../components/charts/CostBars.astro';
import DecompositionFigure from '../components/charts/DecompositionFigure.astro';
import GapDiagram from '../components/charts/GapDiagram.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Install — PACE"
  description="Install the PACE library from vcpkg, Conan or a checkout, and the analyzer from source. Or skip both and use the hosted service."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="section-label">Install</div>
      <h1>Three ways in, and the shortest one needs no toolchain</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The library is a C and C++ component with a CMake package, a vcpkg port and a Conan recipe.
        The analyzer is a Rust binary with no runtime dependencies beyond the Rust standard library.
        If you only want to see what PACE says about a trace you already have, the
        {' '}<a href={href('service/')}>hosted analyzer</a> does it over HTTP.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The library</div>
      <h2>Link it into your profiler</h2>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-package"></i> vcpkg</h3>
          <pre><code>{`git clone ${site.repo}
vcpkg install pace \\
  --overlay-ports=./PACE/ports`}</code></pre>
          <p>The port installs from the published release, so the version you get is the version that was measured.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-cube"></i> Conan</h3>
          <pre><code>{`conan install \\
  --requires=pace/{site.version}`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The recipe was verified end to end with <span class="mono">conan create</span>, and a
            separate consumer project builds against the installed tree and runs.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-wrench"></i> From a checkout</h3>
          <pre><code>{`git clone ${site.repo}
cd PACE/pace-sdk
cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja
cmake --build build -j 8
ctest --test-dir build`}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top:32px">Then find it and link it</h3>
      <pre><code>{`find_package(pace REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(your_target PRIVATE pace::pace)`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        One static or shared library with a stable C ABI. No runtime dependencies beyond the
        platform's threading library, and no elevated privilege for tier 0.
      </p>
      <CostBars />
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The analyzer</div>
      <h2>Or read a trace</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel terminal-block">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-terminal-window"></i> Build and run</h3>
          <pre><code>{`git clone ${site.repo}
cd PACE/pace-analyzer
cargo build --release
./target/release/pace --help`}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-list-checks"></i> The four commands</h3>
          <pre><code>{`pace analyze <trace>    # the attribution report
pace inspect <trace>   # raw per-thread samples
pace verify <trace>    # the decomposition invariant
pace convert <in> --to pace|chrome`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            <span class="mono">verify</span> exits non-zero when a trace breaks
            {' '}<span class="mono">wall == on_cpu + preempted + blocked</span>, so it works as a CI
            gate. It also exits non-zero on an empty trace: the invariant holds vacuously there, and
            a vacuous pass is a false green.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top:32px">A report, offline, as one file</h3>
      <pre><code>{`pace analyze trace.jsonl --output report.html`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The HTML report opens offline with nothing to fetch: the per-thread split and the
        distribution are drawn as inline SVG, so there is no charting library to load and no network
        dependency in a report you are about to attach to a bug.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Verify the install</div>
      <h2>What you should see</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">pace analyze</span> on the recorded contended run prints a summary line, one
        row per thread, and a ranked findings list. The run below is
        {' '}{threads.length} threads that each lost roughly
        {' '}{pct(share(run.totals.preemptedNs, run.totals.wallNs))} of their wall time to preemption,
        which is what the two figures show.
      </p>
      <GapDiagram />
      <DecompositionFigure />
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">If the decomposition says unavailable</div>
        <p>
          That is a real answer, not a failure. It means either the platform cannot make the split
          without privilege, or the kernel is not collecting scheduler statistics. Both cases are
          detected rather than papered over, and the analyzer reports the split as unavailable instead
          of printing a confident zero.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The recorded run in the figures above is
        {' '}{decomposition.samples.toLocaleString('en-US')} samples; the analyzer's own committed
        terminal report covers a larger one. Both are in the repository, and every figure on this
        site is generated from them at build time rather than typed into the page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Troubleshooting</div>
      <h2>Three things that go wrong, and what they mean</h2>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="flow-row">
          <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-shield-slash" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
          <div>
            <h3><span class="mono">perf_event_paranoid</span> is too high for tier 1</h3>
            <p>
              Then tier 1 is unavailable and tier 0 is unaffected. Tier 1 is an upgrade, never a
              requirement — a product that requires root is unshippable inside somebody else's engine.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-row">
          <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-gauge" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
          <div>
            <h3>The preempted bucket reads zero on every thread</h3>
            <p>
              Check whether the kernel is collecting scheduler statistics. PACE detects this and
              reports the split as unavailable, but a system where <span class="mono">schedstat</span>
              is switched off is a system where the three-way split cannot be measured at all.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-row">
          <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-warning" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
          <div>
            <h3>The eBPF tier will not attach</h3>
            <p>
              It needs <span class="mono">CAP_BPF</span>-class privilege and BTF. It is an addition:
              when it cannot attach, the measurement is unchanged and the session reports that it did
              not attach rather than failing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Anything else: <a href={site.issues}>open an issue</a>, or email
        {' '}<a href={`mailto:${site.contact}`}>{site.contact}</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
